-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 1: Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for Building Automation
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for Building Automation',
  'modbus-rtu-tcp-integration-building-automation',
  'Practical guide to integrating Modbus RTU and Modbus TCP devices into BAS, covering register mapping, byte order, polling configuration, and common field issues.',
  E'# Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for Building Automation\n\nModbus remains one of the most common protocols in building automation for connecting meters, VFDs, boilers, chillers, and third-party equipment. Understanding the difference between RTU and TCP, how to read a register map, and how to troubleshoot common integration failures saves significant field time.\n\n## Modbus RTU vs Modbus TCP\n\n| Feature | Modbus RTU | Modbus TCP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Physical layer | RS-485 serial (2-wire or 4-wire) | Ethernet (TCP/IP) |\n| Addressing | Device address 1–247 on serial bus | IP address + Unit ID |\n| Speed | 9600–115200 baud (typically 9600 or 19200) | 10/100 Mbps Ethernet |\n| Distance | Up to 1200 m (4000 ft) per RS-485 segment | Standard Ethernet distances |\n| Max devices per segment | 32 without repeaters (247 addressable) | Limited by network bandwidth |\n| Error checking | CRC-16 | TCP checksums |\n| Common use | Submeters, VFDs, small controllers | Larger meters, gateways, chillers |\n\n## Register Types\n\nModbus defines four data areas. The register type determines whether data is read-only or read-write and whether it is single-bit or 16-bit.\n\n| Register Type | Address Range | Access | Size | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coils | 00001–09999 | Read/Write | 1 bit | Relay outputs, commands |\n| Discrete Inputs | 10001–19999 | Read Only | 1 bit | Status contacts, switches |\n| Input Registers | 30001–39999 | Read Only | 16 bits | Sensor readings, measurements |\n| Holding Registers | 40001–49999 | Read/Write | 16 bits | Setpoints, configuration |\n\n### Address Offset Convention\n\nModbus documentation varies between 0-based and 1-based addressing. A register listed as 40001 in the vendor manual may need to be addressed as register 0 (zero-based) in the BAS driver configuration. Always confirm which convention the BAS platform and the device manual use.\n\n## Reading a Vendor Register Map\n\nEvery Modbus device ships with a register map document. Before configuring any integration:\n\n1. Obtain the register map for the exact device model and firmware version\n2. Identify which function codes the device supports (typically FC03 for holding registers, FC04 for input registers)\n3. Note the data type for each register: unsigned 16-bit, signed 16-bit, 32-bit float (IEEE 754), or 32-bit integer\n4. Check byte order (big-endian vs little-endian) and word order for multi-register values\n5. Record the engineering units and any scaling factors\n\n### Multi-Register Values\n\nValues larger than 16 bits span two consecutive registers. The order of the high and low words varies by manufacturer:\n\n| Word Order | Register N | Register N+1 | Also Called |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Big-endian (AB CD) | High word | Low word | Most common in BAS devices |\n| Little-endian (CD AB) | Low word | High word | Some European manufacturers |\n| Byte-swapped (BA DC) | High word (bytes swapped) | Low word (bytes swapped) | Rare but exists |\n\nIf a 32-bit float reads as an implausible number (very large, very small, or NaN), the word order or byte order is the first thing to check.\n\n## BAS Integration Steps\n\n### Step 1: Physical Connection\n\nFor Modbus RTU:\n- Connect RS-485 A (+) and B (-) terminals consistently across all devices\n- Apply 120-ohm termination resistors at the two physical ends of the bus\n- Ensure consistent baud rate, parity, and stop bits across all devices on the segment\n- Verify each device has a unique Modbus address\n\nFor Modbus TCP:\n- Assign static IP addresses or DHCP reservations to each device\n- Confirm TCP port (default 502) is accessible from the BAS controller\n- Note the Unit ID if using a Modbus TCP gateway that bridges to RTU devices\n\n### Step 2: BAS Driver Configuration\n\nConfigure the Modbus driver on the BAS controller:\n\n- Set the correct serial port settings (RTU) or IP address and port (TCP)\n- Configure the device address or Unit ID\n- Set the polling interval (typically 5–60 seconds depending on the application)\n- Map each register to a BAS point with correct data type, scaling, and engineering units\n\n### Step 3: Verify Values\n\nFor each mapped point:\n- Compare the BAS reading to the value shown on the device''s local display\n- Check that engineering units match (e.g., kW vs W, PSI vs kPa)\n- Verify that writable registers accept commands and that the device responds correctly\n- Confirm that multi-register values (32-bit floats) read as plausible numbers\n\n## Common Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| No communication at all | Wrong baud rate, parity, or wiring | Match serial settings to device; check A/B polarity |\n| Intermittent timeouts | Bus too long, missing termination, noise | Add termination resistors; check cable routing away from power |\n| Values read as zero or max | Wrong register address (off-by-one) | Compare 0-based vs 1-based addressing |\n| 32-bit float reads as garbage | Wrong word order or byte order | Try swapping high/low word; check vendor documentation |\n| One device works, others don''t | Address conflict or daisy-chain break | Verify unique addresses; check wiring continuity |\n| Writes don''t take effect | Wrong function code or register is read-only | Confirm FC06/FC16 for writes; check register type |\n\n## Polling and Performance\n\n- Keep polling intervals at 5 seconds or longer for most BAS applications\n- Group contiguous registers into block reads (FC03/FC04) to reduce transaction count\n- Avoid polling registers you do not need; every poll adds bus traffic\n- On RS-485 with many devices, stagger polling to prevent bus contention\n- Monitor the BAS driver''s error/retry counters to detect degrading communication\n\n## Security Considerations\n\nModbus has no built-in authentication or encryption:\n\n- Keep Modbus networks on dedicated segments, separated from IT and internet-facing networks\n- For Modbus TCP, restrict access using firewall rules (source/destination on port 502)\n- Do not expose Modbus TCP devices directly to the internet\n- Log all write commands for audit purposes\n\n## References\n\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus over Serial Line Specification V1.02](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

