-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 2: Understanding and Troubleshooting the BACnet Priority Array
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Understanding and Troubleshooting the BACnet Priority Array',
  'bacnet-priority-array-commanding-reference',
  'Reference guide to the BACnet priority array covering the 16-level command hierarchy, relinquish defaults, common override conflicts, and troubleshooting procedures.',
  E'# Understanding and Troubleshooting the BACnet Priority Array\n\nThe BACnet priority array is the mechanism that determines which command controls a writable BACnet object (such as an Analog Output or Binary Output). Every writable object has a 16-level priority array where level 1 is the highest priority and level 16 is the lowest. Understanding how this array works is essential for diagnosing override conflicts, stuck outputs, and unexpected equipment behavior.\n\n## The 16 Priority Levels\n\nASHRAE Standard 135 defines the following priority levels:\n\n| Priority | Defined Use (per ASHRAE 135) |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety |\n| 3 | Available |\n| 4 | Available |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off |\n| 7 | Available |\n| 8 | Manual Operator |\n| 9 | Available |\n| 10 | Available |\n| 11 | Available |\n| 12 | Available |\n| 13 | Available |\n| 14 | Available |\n| 15 | Available |\n| 16 | Available (commonly used for default/scheduling) |\n\nLevels marked "Available" can be assigned by the system designer. In practice, most BAS deployments use a subset of these levels.\n\n## How the Priority Array Works\n\nThe controller evaluates the priority array from level 1 down to level 16. The first level that contains a non-NULL value wins and determines the present value of the object.\n\nIf all 16 levels are NULL, the object uses its Relinquish Default value.\n\n### Example\n\n| Priority | Value | Active? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–7 | NULL | No |\n| 8 | 72.0 | YES (this wins) |\n| 9–15 | NULL | No |\n| 16 | 68.0 | No (lower priority) |\n\nPresent Value = 72.0 (from priority 8, Manual Operator)\n\nIf priority 8 is relinquished (set to NULL), the present value falls through to priority 16 (68.0). If priority 16 is also NULL, the Relinquish Default takes effect.\n\n## Common Priority Usage Patterns\n\n| Priority | Common Assignment | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Life safety commands | Fire alarm shutdown override |\n| 2 | Automatic safety | Freeze protection override |\n| 5 | Critical equipment | Chiller plant emergency sequence |\n| 8 | Manual operator override | Operator commands from BAS front-end |\n| 14 | Scheduling | Occupied/unoccupied schedule |\n| 16 | Default control | Normal automatic control loop output |\n\n## Writing to the Priority Array\n\nBACnet Write Property requests specify a priority level. If no priority is specified, the write defaults to priority 16.\n\nTo release a command at a specific priority, write a NULL value at that priority level. This is called relinquishing.\n\n### Write Examples\n\n| Action | BACnet Operation |\n|---|---|\n| Override AO to 75% at priority 8 | WriteProperty(Present_Value, 75.0, priority=8) |\n| Release the override at priority 8 | WriteProperty(Present_Value, NULL, priority=8) |\n| Set schedule value at priority 14 | WriteProperty(Present_Value, 68.0, priority=14) |\n\n## Troubleshooting Priority Array Issues\n\n### Output Stuck at Unexpected Value\n\nThe most common priority array problem: an output is stuck and does not respond to normal commands.\n\n1. Read the full priority array of the object (all 16 levels)\n2. Identify which level has a non-NULL value at the highest priority\n3. Determine what system or operator wrote that value\n4. Decide whether to relinquish (NULL) that level or address the condition that caused the write\n\n### Common Stuck-Value Causes\n\n| Cause | Symptom | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Operator override not released | Value locked at priority 8 | Relinquish priority 8 (write NULL) |\n| BAS program writing at high priority | Control loop ineffective at priority 16 | Fix program to write at correct priority |\n| Multiple systems commanding same object | Unpredictable value changes | Document ownership; assign distinct priorities |\n| Fire/safety override latched | Equipment locked off/on | Verify safety condition cleared; relinquish safety priority |\n| Integration writing at wrong priority | Third-party system overrides local control | Correct the integration''s write priority |\n\n### Relinquish Default Issues\n\nIf the entire priority array is NULL and the output goes to an unexpected value, check the Relinquish Default property. This value is the fallback when no commands are active.\n\n- For binary objects: Relinquish Default is typically Inactive (off)\n- For analog objects: Relinquish Default should be set to a safe operating value\n- If Relinquish Default is 0 and the object controls a damper, the damper closes when all commands are relinquished\n\n## Best Practices\n\n### Document Priority Ownership\n\nCreate a priority assignment table for each project:\n\n| Priority | Owner | Purpose | Release Condition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Fire alarm system | Emergency shutdown | Fire alarm clears |\n| 2 | Freeze protection | Heating override | Temperature above threshold |\n| 8 | Operator | Manual override | Operator releases or time limit |\n| 14 | Schedule | Occupied/unoccupied | Schedule transitions |\n| 16 | Control program | Normal control output | Continuous |\n\n### Avoid Priority Conflicts\n\n- Never have two independent systems write at the same priority level to the same object\n- Use the lowest effective priority for each function so higher-priority overrides can still take effect\n- Do not write at priority 1 or 2 unless the command is genuinely life-safety related\n- Implement timed auto-release for operator overrides to prevent indefinite stuck values\n\n### Reading the Priority Array Programmatically\n\nBACnet provides the Priority_Array property (property ID 87) as a read-only array of 16 values. Reading this property shows the complete state of all active commands on the object, which is the definitive way to diagnose command conflicts.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020 - BACnet: A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Overview](https://bacnet.org/bacnet-overview/)\n- [ASHRAE SSPC 135 - BACnet Committee](https://bacnet.org/)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

