-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 3: VFD Integration over BACnet and Modbus in BAS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'VFD Integration over BACnet and Modbus in BAS',
  'vfd-integration-bacnet-modbus-bas',
  'Field guide to integrating variable frequency drives into building automation systems via BACnet and Modbus, covering command ownership, speed references, fault handling, and safety interlocks.',
  E'# VFD Integration over BACnet and Modbus in BAS\n\nVariable frequency drives (VFDs) control motor speed in fans, pumps, and compressors across nearly every commercial building. Integrating them into the BAS via BACnet or Modbus enables remote monitoring, speed control, energy tracking, and alarm management. This guide covers the practical steps for planning and commissioning VFD network integration.\n\n## VFD Communication Options\n\n| Protocol | Connection | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| BACnet MS/TP | RS-485 daisy chain | Drives on BACnet trunk alongside controllers |\n| BACnet/IP | Ethernet | Larger drives with IP interfaces |\n| Modbus RTU | RS-485 serial | Most common VFD protocol worldwide |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet | IP-capable drives or via gateway |\n| Proprietary | Varies | Vendor-specific drive buses (e.g., ABB FENA, Danfoss FC Protocol) |\n\nBACnet and Modbus are the most common BAS integration paths. The choice depends on the drive manufacturer, existing network infrastructure, and BAS platform.\n\n## Planning the Integration\n\n### Key Points to Map\n\nAt minimum, map the following for each VFD:\n\n| Point | Direction | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run/Stop command | Write | Start and stop the drive |\n| Speed reference | Write | Set speed (% or Hz) |\n| Run status | Read | Confirm drive is running |\n| Actual speed | Read | Feedback of current speed |\n| Motor current | Read | Load monitoring |\n| Fault status | Read | Drive is in fault condition |\n| Fault code | Read | Specific fault identification |\n| Fault reset | Write | Remote reset after fault |\n| Power/energy | Read | Energy consumption (if available) |\n\n### Command Ownership\n\nBefore sending any run or speed commands from the BAS, establish who controls the drive:\n\n| Control Source | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Hand (local) | Operator presses buttons on the drive keypad |\n| Off | Drive is disabled regardless of BAS commands |\n| Auto (remote) | BAS has command authority via network |\n\nThe drive''s Hand-Off-Auto (HOA) switch or parameter determines which source is active. The BAS should only command the drive when it is in Auto mode. Read the HOA status from the drive to confirm command authority before issuing run or speed commands.\n\n## BACnet VFD Integration\n\nDrives with native BACnet support expose standard BACnet objects:\n\n| BACnet Object | Typical Mapping |\n|---|---|\n| Binary Output | Run/Stop command |\n| Analog Output | Speed reference (0–100% or Hz) |\n| Binary Input | Run status, fault status |\n| Analog Input | Actual speed, motor current, power |\n| Multi-State Input | Operating mode, fault code |\n\n### BACnet Priority Considerations\n\nWhen writing Run/Stop or Speed commands via BACnet, assign appropriate priority levels:\n- Safety shutdowns: Priority 1 or 2\n- Normal BAS control: Priority 16 or a project-defined level\n- Operator overrides: Priority 8\n\nRefer to the BACnet priority array documentation for the project''s priority assignment table.\n\n## Modbus VFD Integration\n\n### Register Mapping\n\nEvery VFD manufacturer publishes a Modbus register map. Common register categories:\n\n| Category | Registers | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Control word | Holding register (FC06/FC16) | Bit-mapped: run, stop, direction, reset |\n| Speed reference | Holding register (FC06/FC16) | Typically 0–10000 = 0.0–100.0% (scaled by 100) |\n| Status word | Input register (FC04) | Bit-mapped: running, fault, at speed, warning |\n| Actual speed | Input register (FC04) | Same scaling as speed reference |\n| Current | Input register (FC04) | Usually in 0.1 A or 0.01 A resolution |\n| Fault code | Input register (FC04) | Manufacturer-specific codes |\n\n### Control Word Bit Mapping\n\nThe control word is typically a 16-bit register where individual bits trigger commands. The exact bit mapping varies by manufacturer, but a common pattern:\n\n| Bit | Function |\n|---|---|\n| 0 | Run (1 = start, 0 = stop) |\n| 1 | Direction (0 = forward, 1 = reverse) |\n| 2 | Jog |\n| 3 | Fault reset (rising edge) |\n| 4–15 | Manufacturer-specific |\n\nAlways verify the control word definition against the specific drive model documentation.\n\n### Scaling\n\nModbus registers are integer values. Drives typically scale speed references:\n- 0 = 0.0%\n- 10000 = 100.0%\n- Scale factor = 0.01\n\nThe BAS must apply the same scaling factor to convert between engineering units and raw register values.\n\n## Commissioning Procedure\n\n1. Confirm the drive''s communication parameters match the BAS driver (baud rate, parity, address for RTU; IP and port for TCP)\n2. Verify the drive is set to accept network commands (often a drive parameter like "Command Source = Fieldbus")\n3. Read status and feedback points first to confirm communication works\n4. Test the run command with the motor uncoupled or under safe conditions\n5. Verify speed reference tracks correctly from 0% to 100%\n6. Trigger a fault and confirm the BAS receives the fault status and code\n7. Test the fault reset command from the BAS\n8. Confirm HOA behavior: drive should ignore BAS commands when switched to Hand or Off\n9. Verify that safety interlocks (freeze protection, fire alarm, high static pressure) override the BAS speed command\n\n## Safety Interlocks\n\nVFD integration must respect hardwired safety interlocks:\n\n- Hardwired safety stops (e.g., fire alarm relay, smoke detector) must bypass the network and shut down the drive regardless of BAS commands\n- The BAS should monitor the safety interlock status but must not be the sole safety shutdown path\n- After a safety event, the drive should require a deliberate reset before restarting\n\n## Common Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| No communication | Wrong baud rate, address, or wiring | Match drive comm parameters to BAS driver |\n| BAS sends run but drive doesn''t start | Drive not in Auto/Remote mode | Check HOA switch and command source parameter |\n| Speed command has no effect | Speed reference source not set to network | Set drive parameter for fieldbus speed reference |\n| Drive faults immediately on start | Safety interlock active or motor issue | Check hardwired interlocks and motor connections |\n| Speed reads 0 while drive is running | Wrong register or scaling | Verify register address and scale factor |\n| Fault reset doesn''t work | Reset requires rising edge, not held value | Pulse the reset bit (set to 1 then back to 0) |\n\n## References\n\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020 - BACnet Standard](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Overview](https://bacnet.org/bacnet-overview/)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

