-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 4: BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization',
  'bas-alarm-management-rationalization',
  'Best practices for organizing, prioritizing, and rationalizing BAS alarms to reduce alarm fatigue, improve operator response, and separate life safety from nuisance notifications.',
  E'# BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization\n\nAlarm fatigue is one of the most common operational problems in building automation. When operators receive hundreds of alarms per day, critical events get buried in noise. Alarm rationalization is the structured process of reviewing every alarm, assigning appropriate priority and response, and eliminating nuisance notifications.\n\n## The Problem: Alarm Overload\n\nTypical symptoms of an unmanaged alarm system:\n\n- Operators acknowledge alarms in bulk without reading them\n- Critical equipment failures are missed because they look like routine notifications\n- The same alarms fire and clear repeatedly (chattering)\n- Alarms are configured with default thresholds that do not match actual operating conditions\n- Nobody knows which alarms require an immediate response vs. a next-day work order\n\n## Alarm Classification\n\nISA-18.2 (Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries) provides a framework that applies well to BAS. Classify every alarm into one of these categories:\n\n| Category | Response Time | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Life Safety | Immediate (automated + operator) | Fire alarm, smoke detected in duct |\n| Equipment Protection | Immediate (within minutes) | Chiller high-pressure lockout, freeze stat trip |\n| Operational | Prompt (within the hour) | Discharge air temperature out of range |\n| Maintenance | Planned (next business day) | Filter differential pressure high |\n| Informational / Status | No action required | Equipment start/stop logging |\n\nInformational events should not generate operator alarms. They belong in event logs, not alarm queues.\n\n## Alarm Priority Levels\n\nDefine a consistent priority scheme across the entire BAS:\n\n| Priority | Definition | Expected Response |\n|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Risk to life safety or major equipment damage | Immediate automated response + operator notification |\n| High | Equipment protection or significant comfort/process impact | Operator response within 15 minutes |\n| Medium | Operational deviation requiring attention | Operator response within 1 hour |\n| Low | Maintenance condition, no immediate impact | Work order generated, addressed within days |\n\n## Rationalization Process\n\n### Step 1: Inventory All Alarms\n\nExtract a complete list of configured alarms from the BAS:\n- Point name and description\n- Current alarm thresholds (high, low, limits)\n- Current priority setting\n- Current routing (who receives it)\n\n### Step 2: Review Each Alarm\n\nFor each alarm, answer these questions:\n\n1. What equipment or condition does this alarm represent?\n2. Is operator action required when this alarm fires?\n3. What is the consequence if this alarm is ignored?\n4. What specific action should the operator take?\n5. Is the threshold appropriate for actual operating conditions?\n6. Does this alarm chatter (fire and clear repeatedly)?\n\n### Step 3: Assign Priority and Response\n\nBased on the review:\n- Assign a priority level from the table above\n- Write a clear alarm message that tells the operator what happened and what to do\n- Set routing based on priority (critical alarms may go to phone/SMS; low-priority to email or dashboard only)\n- Set appropriate time delays to prevent chattering\n\n### Step 4: Eliminate Nuisance Alarms\n\nCommon sources of nuisance alarms:\n\n| Source | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Threshold too tight | Widen deadband or adjust setpoint to match actual conditions |\n| Alarm on startup transient | Add time delay (e.g., suppress for 5 minutes after equipment start) |\n| Alarm on scheduled event | Suppress during known schedule transitions |\n| Sensor drift or noise | Fix the sensor; do not mask it with wider thresholds |\n| Duplicate alarms | Remove redundant alarm on same condition |\n| Alarms on informational events | Reclassify as log events, remove from alarm queue |\n\n## Alarm Configuration Best Practices\n\n### Deadband and Delay\n\n- Apply deadband to analog alarms so they do not chatter at the threshold boundary\n- Use on-delay timers so transient conditions do not trigger alarms\n- Typical deadband: 2–5% of the alarm threshold range\n- Typical delay: 30 seconds to 5 minutes depending on the process\n\n### Alarm Text\n\nWrite alarm messages that help the operator respond:\n\n| Bad | Good |\n|---|---|\n| AHU-1 ALARM | AHU-1 Supply Air Temp High (above 85°F) – Check cooling valve and compressor status |\n| FAULT | Chiller-2 High Head Pressure Lockout – Do not restart until condenser is checked |\n| ZONE TEMP | Floor 3 East Zone Temp Low (below 65°F) – Check VAV damper and reheat operation |\n\n### Alarm Routing\n\n| Priority | Routing |\n|---|---|\n| Critical | BAS dashboard + SMS/phone + building operations |\n| High | BAS dashboard + email to on-duty operator |\n| Medium | BAS dashboard + email summary |\n| Low | BAS dashboard only; included in daily/weekly report |\n\n## Ongoing Alarm Management\n\nRationalization is not a one-time project. Establish a recurring review:\n\n| Activity | Frequency |\n|---|---|\n| Review top 10 most frequent alarms | Monthly |\n| Review unacknowledged alarms older than 24 hours | Weekly |\n| Audit alarm priorities after system changes | Per project |\n| Full alarm rationalization review | Annually |\n| Review alarm response procedures with operators | Semi-annually |\n\n## Metrics to Track\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Alarms per operator per day | Below 150 (per ISA-18.2 guidance) |\n| Percentage of alarms acknowledged within target time | Above 90% |\n| Standing alarms (active and unacknowledged > 24 hours) | Below 10 |\n| Chattering alarms (fire > 5 times/day) | Zero |\n| Nuisance alarm percentage | Below 10% of total |\n\n## References\n\n- [ISA-18.2-2016 - Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries](https://www.isa.org/products/ansi-isa-18-2-2016-management-of-alarm-systems-for)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015 - Specifying Building Automation Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/ashrae-guideline-13-2015)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

