-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 5: Niagara Workbench Connection Troubleshooting
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'troubleshooting'),
  'Niagara Workbench Connection Troubleshooting',
  'niagara-workbench-connection-troubleshooting',
  'Systematic troubleshooting guide for Niagara Workbench connection failures covering network reachability, protocol/port mismatches, certificate issues, and authentication problems.',
  E'# Niagara Workbench Connection Troubleshooting\n\nFailing to connect Workbench to a Niagara station or platform is the most common field support issue. The causes range from basic network problems to TLS certificate mismatches to credential lockouts. This guide provides a systematic approach to diagnosing the specific failure.\n\n## Connection Architecture\n\nWorkbench connects to Niagara stations using one of these protocols:\n\n| Protocol | Port | Transport | Use Case |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fox | 1911 | TCP (unencrypted) | Legacy; not recommended for production |\n| foxs | 4911 | TLS over TCP | Standard secure connection |\n| http | 80 | TCP | Web browser (unencrypted) |\n| https | 443 | TLS over TCP | Web browser (encrypted) |\n\nModern Niagara installations (4.x) default to foxs and https. The connection URL format is:\n\n```\nfoxs://[host]:[port]/\nhttps://[host]:[port]/\n```\n\n## Step 1: Verify Network Reachability\n\nBefore troubleshooting Niagara-specific issues, confirm basic network connectivity:\n\n1. Can you ping the station IP address?\n2. Is the station on the same subnet, or is routing configured correctly?\n3. Is there a firewall between your laptop and the station?\n4. If connecting via VPN, is the VPN tunnel established?\n\n| Test | Command | Expected Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ping | ping [station-ip] | Reply received |\n| Port check (foxs) | Test connection to port 4911 | Port open / connection accepted |\n| Port check (https) | Test connection to port 443 | Port open / connection accepted |\n\nIf ping succeeds but the port check fails, a firewall is blocking the specific port.\n\n## Step 2: Confirm Protocol and Port\n\nMismatch between the protocol Workbench is using and what the station expects is a frequent cause of connection failure:\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause |\n|---|---|\n| "Connection refused" | Wrong port or station not listening on that port |\n| Connection hangs then times out | Firewall silently dropping packets on that port |\n| "Unexpected message format" | Connecting with fox to a foxs port (or vice versa) |\n| Browser shows "This site can''t be reached" | Wrong port or HTTPS-only enforcement blocking HTTP |\n\n### Check Which Ports the Station Is Listening On\n\nFrom the station''s platform (if accessible):\n- Platform > Station > fox Port, foxs Port\n- Platform > Web Service > http Port, https Port\n- Check if "Redirect HTTP to HTTPS" is enabled\n\n## Step 3: TLS Certificate Issues\n\nTLS problems are the most common Workbench connection failures on hardened stations.\n\n### Certificate Not Trusted\n\nWhen Workbench encounters a station certificate it has not seen before:\n- Workbench displays a certificate approval dialog\n- If the certificate was previously approved but has since been regenerated, Workbench flags a mismatch\n\nResolution:\n1. Review the certificate details in the Workbench dialog\n2. If the certificate is legitimate (correct station, expected issuer), approve it\n3. If the certificate was regenerated, clear the old entry from Workbench''s trust store and approve the new certificate\n\n### Certificate Expired\n\nNiagara self-signed certificates have an expiration date. After expiry:\n- Workbench may refuse to connect or show a warning\n- Browser connections show certificate error pages\n\nResolution:\n1. Connect to the platform (which may use a separate certificate)\n2. Regenerate the station certificate from Platform > Certificate Management\n3. Restart the station\n4. Re-approve the new certificate in Workbench\n\n### Clock Skew\n\nIf the station clock or the laptop clock is significantly wrong, TLS handshakes fail because certificates appear "not yet valid" or "expired" based on the wrong time.\n\nResolution: Correct the system clock on both the station and the laptop. Enable NTP on the station.\n\n## Step 4: Authentication Failures\n\n### Wrong Credentials\n\n| Symptom | Cause |\n|---|---|\n| "Authentication failed" | Wrong username or password |\n| "Account locked" | Too many failed attempts |\n| "Account expired" | Password expiration policy triggered |\n\n### Platform vs Station Credentials\n\nNiagara has two credential domains:\n- **Platform credentials**: Used to connect to the platform daemon (port 3011 by default)\n- **Station credentials**: Used to connect to the station via Workbench or web browser\n\nConfirm you are using the correct credential set for the connection type.\n\n### Account Lockout Recovery\n\nIf the platform admin account is locked:\n1. Physically access the controller\n2. Navigate to https://[controller-ip]:3011/recover in a browser\n3. Enter the controller''s serial number (printed on the hardware label)\n4. Create a new platform admin account\n\nThis recovery feature is available on Niagara 4.4+ and requires physical access to the serial number.\n\n## Step 5: Station Not Running\n\nIf the station process is not running, Workbench cannot connect:\n\n| Symptom | Check |\n|---|---|\n| Platform connects but station does not | Station may be stopped; check platform Station status |\n| Platform shows station in "Fault" | Station crashed; check platform logs |\n| Neither platform nor station connects | Controller may be rebooting or powered off |\n\nResolution:\n1. Connect to the platform\n2. Check station status (Running, Stopped, Fault)\n3. If stopped, start the station from the platform\n4. If in Fault, review the station log for errors before restarting\n\n## Step 6: Workbench Version Mismatch\n\nWorkbench version should be compatible with the station version:\n\n| Scenario | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Workbench version matches station | Normal operation |\n| Workbench newer than station | Usually works (backward compatible) |\n| Workbench older than station | May fail or show unexpected behavior |\n\nFor best results, use a Workbench version equal to or newer than the station version.\n\n## Quick Reference: Connection Failure Decision Tree\n\n1. Can you ping the station? **No** → Fix network/routing\n2. Is the correct port open? **No** → Check firewall rules and station port configuration\n3. Does Workbench show a certificate error? **Yes** → Approve, regenerate, or fix clock\n4. Does authentication fail? **Yes** → Verify credentials, check lockout, confirm platform vs station\n5. Does the platform connect but not the station? **Yes** → Station may be stopped or faulted\n6. None of the above? → Check Workbench version compatibility and review station logs\n\n## References\n\n- [Tridium - Niagara Documentation Library](https://www.tridium.com/us/en/services-support/library)\n- [Tridium Niagara 4 Platform Guide - Connection and Security Configuration](https://www.tridium.com/us/en/services-support/library)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT a.id, f.id
FROM wiki_articles a
CROSS JOIN wiki_facets f
WHERE a.slug = 'niagara-workbench-connection-troubleshooting'
  AND f.slug IN ('niagara', 'troubleshooting', 'networking', 'security', 'tls', 'certificate')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 6: ASHRAE Guideline 36 High-Performance Sequences for BAS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'ASHRAE Guideline 36: High-Performance Sequences for BAS',
  'ashrae-guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-bas',
  'Overview of ASHRAE Guideline 36 standardized HVAC control sequences for BAS professionals, covering key sequence concepts, implementation considerations, and trim-and-respond logic.',
  E'# ASHRAE Guideline 36: High-Performance Sequences for BAS\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 provides standardized, high-performance sequences of operation for HVAC systems. For BAS professionals, it represents a shift from project-specific custom sequences toward published, peer-reviewed control logic that can be consistently implemented, commissioned, and maintained.\n\n## What Guideline 36 Covers\n\nGuideline 36 defines sequences for common HVAC system types:\n\n| System Type | Key Sequences |\n|---|---|\n| Single-duct VAV AHUs | Supply air temperature control, static pressure control, outdoor air control, economizer |\n| VAV terminal units | Cooling-only, reheat, fan-powered, dual-duct |\n| Dual-duct AHUs | Hot deck and cold deck control |\n| Multiple-zone VAV | Minimum outdoor air, zone reset logic |\n| Chilled water plants | Chiller staging, condenser water control, differential pressure reset |\n| Hot water plants | Boiler staging, supply temperature reset |\n| Dedicated outdoor air systems | Supply temperature control, dehumidification |\n\n## Why Guideline 36 Matters for BAS\n\n### Before Guideline 36\n\n- Every project had custom sequences written by the design engineer or controls contractor\n- Sequence quality varied widely depending on the author\n- Commissioning agents had no published standard to compare against\n- Operators could not transfer sequence knowledge between buildings\n\n### With Guideline 36\n\n- Sequences are published, peer-reviewed, and regularly updated\n- Commissioning can verify against a known reference\n- BAS programmers can implement from a consistent specification\n- Energy performance expectations are documented\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### Trim-and-Respond Reset Logic\n\nGuideline 36 uses trim-and-respond (T&R) logic instead of traditional PID-based resets for supply air temperature and static pressure setpoints. T&R resets the setpoint based on zone demand signals rather than a single duct sensor.\n\nHow it works:\n1. Each zone sends a demand request (0 = satisfied, 1+ = needs more cooling/heating/pressure)\n2. The AHU counts the number of zones with active requests\n3. If requests are below the threshold, the setpoint trims toward a more energy-efficient value (higher supply air temp, lower static pressure)\n4. If requests exceed the threshold, the setpoint responds toward a less efficient but more effective value\n5. The result is a setpoint that continuously adapts to actual zone loads\n\nBenefits:\n- Saves energy by avoiding fixed, worst-case setpoints\n- Responds to actual demand rather than a single sensor location\n- Reduces simultaneous heating and cooling\n\n### Zone-Level Request Generation\n\nGuideline 36 VAV terminal unit sequences generate requests that feed the AHU trim-and-respond logic:\n\n| Request Type | Generated When |\n|---|---|\n| Cooling request | Zone temperature above cooling setpoint and damper near full open |\n| Heating request | Zone temperature below heating setpoint and reheat valve near full open |\n| Pressure request | Damper near full open but insufficient airflow |\n\nThese requests are integers (0, 1, 2, 3) indicating the severity of the zone''s need.\n\n### Setpoint Schedules and Deadbands\n\nGuideline 36 defines occupied, unoccupied, standby, and warmup/cooldown operating modes with specific setpoint adjustments and deadbands for each mode.\n\n| Mode | Cooling Setpoint | Heating Setpoint | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Occupied | 75°F (adjustable) | 70°F (adjustable) | Normal operation |\n| Standby | Occupied + 2°F | Occupied − 2°F | Zones with occupancy sensors showing unoccupied |\n| Unoccupied | 85°F | 55°F | Setback/setup for energy savings |\n| Warmup | N/A | 70°F | Morning warmup before occupancy |\n| Cooldown | 75°F | N/A | Morning cooldown before occupancy |\n\n### Economizer Sequences\n\nGuideline 36 provides detailed economizer control sequences including:\n- High-limit shutoff strategies (fixed dry-bulb, differential dry-bulb, fixed enthalpy, differential enthalpy)\n- Minimum outdoor air control using measured airflow or damper position\n- Integrated economizer operation with mechanical cooling\n\n## Implementation Considerations\n\n### BAS Programming\n\nImplementing Guideline 36 requires:\n- Zone-level demand request calculation in each VAV controller\n- Communication of request values from zone controllers to AHU controllers\n- Trim-and-respond logic blocks in AHU programming\n- Mode determination logic (occupied, unoccupied, standby, warmup, cooldown)\n- Proper coordination between economizer, mechanical cooling, and heating stages\n\n### Network Traffic\n\nTrim-and-respond logic requires zone controllers to communicate request values to the AHU controller. Plan for:\n- BACnet COV subscriptions or regular polling of request values\n- Sufficient network bandwidth for all zone-to-AHU communication\n- Fallback behavior if communication to a zone controller is lost\n\n### Commissioning Guideline 36 Sequences\n\nCommissioning should verify:\n- Zone request generation responds correctly to load conditions\n- Trim-and-respond logic resets setpoints within documented ranges\n- Mode transitions occur at the correct times and conditions\n- Economizer sequences operate correctly across all outdoor air conditions\n- Energy performance aligns with design expectations\n\n### Common Implementation Challenges\n\n| Challenge | Guidance |\n|---|---|\n| Legacy controllers lacking request support | Upgrade zone controllers or use simplified request generation |\n| Network bandwidth for zone requests | Use COV subscriptions with appropriate deadbands |\n| Operator unfamiliarity with T&R logic | Train operators on the concept; document expected setpoint ranges |\n| Mixing Guideline 36 with custom sequences | Document deviations clearly; do not partially implement T&R |\n| Setpoint range tuning | Start with Guideline 36 defaults; adjust based on commissioning data |\n\n## Guideline 36 and Energy Codes\n\nASHRAE Standard 90.1 and many energy codes reference Guideline 36 sequences. Some jurisdictions require Guideline 36 compliance for new construction. Check local code requirements before finalizing the sequence of operation.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021 - High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-of-operation-for-hvac-systems)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 90.1 - Energy Standard for Sites and Buildings](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standard-90-1)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020 - BACnet](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

